// Crusaders Mainframe — retrying a relay-affecting frame the Mega did not take.
//
// The link is 9600 baud beside four relay coils, and the Mega answers a frame
// whose checksum did not survive it with `!ERR,BADCRC` — or, for a line that
// overran its buffer, `!EVT,OVERRUN,RX`, or, for a line that did not begin with
// the `!` sentinel at all, `!EVT,BADFRAME,RX`. The bridge counted those and did
// nothing. For a `!LCD` that is a missed repaint; for the `!ON` that lights a
// paid court it is a member on a dark court with a screen saying "COURT B 45
// MIN", and a `!STATE` two seconds later synthesising a Release for a session
// that never started.
//
// WHY BADFRAME COUNTS TOO
// -----------------------
// The production Mega runs proto::LegacyDialect::Refuse, so a line is
// classified by its first byte: a glitch byte landing on an idle line ahead of
// the frame makes `X!ON,B,2700*CC` a BADFRAME, not a BADCRC. The link is idle
// far more than it is busy, so that is the MORE likely noise-induced loss of an
// `!ON`, and it was the one this type excluded (review 3, finding 13).
//
// WHY `!EXT` IS KEPT AS WELL AS `!ON`
// -----------------------------------
// A booking longer than the ninety-minute relay cap is held up by an `!EXT`
// sent once, ~60 s before the cap runs out (bridgelogic::Relight). Losing that
// one frame darkens a court mid-game, and the bridge had no idea: Relight
// stamped the extension the moment the frame left the TX ring and then said
// nothing for another half hour (review 3, finding 7). So the LAST
// relay-affecting downlink is kept, whichever of the two it was.
//
// `!ERR,BADARG` in reply to an `!EXT` is not a transmission failure and is not
// retried: it is the Mega saying the relay is already OPEN — the court is dark
// and there is nothing to extend. See isExtRefusal.
//
// So the last relay-affecting frame is kept, and when the Mega reports a
// receive error inside kRetryWindowMs of it, it is sent ONCE more. Once, not
// until acknowledged: a second failure is a cable, not a transient, and a loop
// of ONs on a broken link is a loop of ONs. Inside the window, not any time: an
// error arriving later than the reconcile grace cannot be about this frame, and
// re-sending an ON for a court that was since released would be lights nobody
// asked for.
//
// The remembered frame carries its COURT, because a release on court A must
// not throw away the retry court B's grant is waiting on (review 3, finding
// 24) — clearIfCourt is what releaseCourt calls.
//
// No Arduino headers, no heap. Compiles for Xtensa and the host.

#ifndef CRUSADERS_BRIDGELOGIC_ONRETRY_H
#define CRUSADERS_BRIDGELOGIC_ONRETRY_H

#include <stdint.h>

#include "../protocol/Protocol.h"
#include "../timebase/TimeBase.h"

namespace bridgelogic {

/// How long after a relay-affecting frame a receive error from the Mega is
/// taken to be about it. A frame is ~20 ms on the wire and the Mega answers
/// within a pass; this is generous, and it is shorter than the reconcile grace
/// on purpose so the retry lands before a `!STATE` could be acted on.
inline constexpr uint32_t kRetryWindowMs = 1500UL;

/// No court. The value clear() leaves behind, and what a frame whose court
/// argument does not parse is remembered under — so clearIfCourt for a real
/// court never matches it.
inline constexpr uint8_t kNoRetryCourt = 0xFFU;

/// True for the uplink reports that mean "your last line did not decode":
/// `!ERR,BADCRC`, `!EVT,OVERRUN,RX` and `!EVT,BADFRAME,RX`. `name` and `detail`
/// are the frame's two arguments. `!EVT,OVERRUN,TX` is the Mega reporting a
/// drop on ITS way out, which says nothing about what it received, and does not
/// count.
bool isReceiveError(proto::Verb verb, const char* name, const char* detail);

/// True for `!ERR,BADARG` — which, following an `!EXT`, is the Mega refusing to
/// extend a relay that is already open. The frame arrived and was understood;
/// the court is simply dark. Re-sending it would be pointless, and treating it
/// as a lost frame is how a bridge ends up believing it extended a session that
/// had already ended.
bool isExtRefusal(proto::Verb verb, const char* name);

/// What an uplink report about the last line the Mega received means for the
/// remembered frame. One verdict, so the composition root cannot get the
/// ordering of the two questions wrong — which it did: the BADARG branch was
/// written first and tested `verb() == Ext` against a memory nothing ever put
/// an `!EXT` into, so it was unreachable, and BADARG fell through to a
/// comparison against "BADCRC" that is false by construction.
enum class ReceiveErrorAction : uint8_t {
  /// Nothing to do. Not a receive error, or nothing worth replaying.
  None = 0U,
  /// `!ERR,BADARG` answering an `!EXT`: the relay is ALREADY OPEN. The frame
  /// arrived and was understood; the court is simply dark. Forget the
  /// extension and let `!STATE` reconcile the court.
  ExtRefused = 1U,
  /// "your last line did not decode", and a relay-affecting frame is
  /// remembered: ask OnRetry::shouldResend.
  Resend = 2U,
};

/// The one place the two questions are asked, in the one order that is
/// correct. `remembered` is OnRetry::verb().
ReceiveErrorAction classifyReceiveError(proto::Verb verb, const char* name, const char* detail,
                                        proto::Verb remembered);

class OnRetry {
public:
  /// A relay-affecting frame (`!ON` or `!EXT`) left the bridge at `nowMs`.
  /// Kept for one retry. Anything else CLEARS the memory rather than replacing
  /// it: a repaint the Mega missed is a repaint, and a memory kept alive past
  /// a frame that is not about a relay is a replay attributed to the wrong
  /// line — see the .cpp, and D1-3.
  ///
  /// THE COMPOSITION ROOT REGISTERS EVERY FRAME IT WRITES, through this one
  /// call in its one write path. Registering only the interesting frames is
  /// what left the memory alive across the `!AUTH`, `!OFF` and `!PING` that
  /// a `!ERR,BADCRC` could equally have been about.
  void sent(const proto::Frame& on, uint32_t nowMs);

  /// The Mega reported a receive error at `nowMs`. True — with `out` set to
  /// the frame to re-send — exactly once per remembered frame, and only inside
  /// the window. False otherwise.
  bool shouldResend(uint32_t nowMs, proto::Frame& out);

  /// Forget the remembered frame: the retry was spent, or the situation it
  /// belonged to is over. Idempotent.
  void clear();

  /// Forget it only when it is about `court`. What a release calls, so that
  /// court A going dark inside the 1.5 s window does not drop the retry court
  /// B's grant is waiting on.
  void clearIfCourt(uint8_t court);

  bool pending() const { return window_.isArmed(); }

  /// The court the remembered frame is about, or kNoRetryCourt.
  uint8_t court() const { return court_; }

  /// The verb of the remembered frame — `!ON`, `!EXT`, or Unknown when there
  /// is nothing remembered. What tells a caller whether a `BADARG` reply is
  /// about an extension.
  proto::Verb verb() const;

  /// Retries sent since boot. Diagnostics: a climbing count is a cable.
  uint16_t retries() const { return retries_; }

private:
  proto::Frame frame_;
  tb::Deadline window_;
  uint8_t court_ = kNoRetryCourt;
  uint16_t retries_ = 0U;
};

} // namespace bridgelogic

#endif // CRUSADERS_BRIDGELOGIC_ONRETRY_H
